response:
{
  "answer": "Yes",
  "reasoning": "If someone is entering a locked backyard storage room at night to remove power tools without permission, it is generally appropriate to report it — especially if it is criminal (unauthorized entry/theft), poses a safety risk (power tools used by an inexperienced person at night), is part of a pattern, or cannot be resolved within the household. That said, context matters: if the child is simply borrowing tools with a misunderstanding, is very young, or this is a one‑time incident, an initial family conversation, returning the items, securing the tools, and addressing underlying causes (discipline, counseling, supervision) may be preferable. If you choose to report, tell authorities only the facts, preserve evidence (photos, timestamps), and be aware that juvenile matters are often handled differently (diversion, family services) depending on local law. In short—report when safety, repeated misconduct, or inability to resolve internally make it necessary; otherwise try non‑police family remedies first."
}

